  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The Appalachian Chanterelle is a small, delicate fungus native to the woodlands of New England and the Appalachian region. It is prized by foragers for its delicate flavor and fruity aroma, though it is less commonly encountered than its larger relatives.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "False Gills",
      "description": "Features blunt, forked ridges running down the stem rather than true gills, which are a hallmark of chanterelle species",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Small Size",
      "description": "Typically 1-3 inches tall with a golden-yellow to orange cap that is often funnel-shaped or trumpet-like",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Fruity Aroma",
      "description": "Emits a distinctive fruity, apricot-like smell that helps distinguish it from similar-looking species",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Found in moist, deciduous and mixed forests of New England, often appearing near oak, beech, and hemlock trees during summer and early fall months.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Unlike mass-produced cultivated mushrooms, wild chanterelles cannot be reliably grown in cultivation, making wild-foraged specimens especially valued by gourmet chefs and foragers.",
